
## 2.3.1

| WT (gene) | Mutated (gene) |
|---|---|
| The gene is located at chromosome locus 21q22.11 and contains five exons. The c.14C>T missense mutation occurs in exon 1. The mRNA encodes a protein of 154 amino acids, and the mutation causes an amino acid substitution, p.Ala5Val. | |

| WT (protein sequence) | Mutated (protein sequence) |
|---|---|
| The mutation changes some physicochemical properties of the protein. The substitution of alanine with valine increases hydrophobicity and molecular volume due to valine's larger, branched side chain. These changes may affect local flexibility and the protein's secondary structure, including alpha-helix formation. | |

| WT (structure) | Mutated (structure) |
|---|---|
| The wild-type protein functions as a homodimer. The increase in side-chain volume caused by the amino acid substitution may affect local structural packing and the dimer interface. Increased hydrophobicity may also promote abnormal protein–protein interactions, protein misfolding, and aggregation. | |

| WT (function) | Mutated (function) |
|---|---|
| The protein's main function is to catalyse the dismutation of superoxide radicals produced during cellular respiration into oxygen and hydrogen peroxide, acting as an antioxidant enzyme. However, the mutation can destabilise the protein and impair its normal function. Mutant SOD1 may acquire toxic properties, promoting misfolding and aggregation. These aggregates and other toxic effects can contribute to motor neuron dysfunction and degeneration, leading to amyotrophic lateral sclerosis type 1 (ALS1). | |
